Rust Day 6: Integers & floats, explained line by line. i32 vs u8, f64 vs f32, why 7 / 2 = 3, and the error you get when you mix them.

📌 In this short:
✅ i = signed (can be negative), u = unsigned (0 and up); the number is the size in bits
✅ Defaults: whole numbers are i32, decimals are f64; 1_000_000 is easier to read
✅ Integer division drops the fraction (7 / 2 = 3); % is the remainder; i32 + f64 = error E0277

💻 Code from the video:
fn main() {
    let a = 42;          // i32
    let b: u8 = 255;     // 0..=255
    let c = 1_000_000;
    let d = 3.5;         // f64
    let e: f32 = 2.0;
    println!("{a} {b} {c}");
    println!("{d} {e}");
}

📖 Learn more: https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/ch03-02-data-types.html
